650, 374, 220, 16, 17, 334, 382, 44364, 14374, 14822, 220, 17, 25, 39633, 279, 479, 9650, 553, 220, 16, 16, 271, 14085, 198, 16, 17, 488, 220, 16, 16, 284, 1124, 79075, 90, 17, 18, 532, 14085, 271, 44364, 14374, 25521, 227, 13023, 21806, 1447, 14085, 198, 59, 79075, 90, 17, 18, 532, 14085, 151645], "output_tokens": 441, "input_tokens": 39, "capped": false, "completion": "We are asked to find the result when the **greatest common factor (GCF)** of **6432** and **132** is **increased by 11**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find the GCF of 6432 and 132\n\nWe can use the **Euclidean algorithm** to find the GCF.\n\n#### Step 1.1: Divide 6432 by 132\n\n$$\n6432 \\div 132 = 48 \\text{ remainder } 96\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n6432 = 132 \\times 48 + 96\n$$\n\n#### Step 1.2: Now find GCF of 132 and 96\n\n$$\n132 \\div 96 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 36\n$$\n\n$$\n132 = 96 \\times 1 + 36\n$$\n\n#### Step 1.3: Now find GCF of 96 and 36\n\n$$\n96 \\div 36 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 24\n$$\n\n$$\n96 = 36 \\times 2 + 24\n$$\n\n#### Step 1.4: Now find GCF of 36 and 24\n\n$$\n36 \\div 24 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 12\n$$\n\n$$\n36 = 24 \\times 1 + 12\n$$\n\n#### Step 1.5: Now find GCF of 24 and 12\n\n$$\n24 \\div 12 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 0\n$$\n\nSo, the **GCF is 12**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Increase the GCF by 11\n\n$$\n12 + 11 = \\boxed{23}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{23}\n$$", "diagnostic_seconds": 11.612501154188067}
